-- Migration v7.1 — engines the curated faults can see
--
-- The recommender demotes a model by the share of its engines on sale in a
-- year that carry an approved critical fault (seeds/known_engine_issues.json,
-- app/services/recommend_service.curated_fault_share); the model pages list
-- the faults of a model's engines. A spot check on 2026-11-17 found a 2018
-- Peugeot 208 at 0% (the catalogue had only 208 II engines, 2019+, so no
-- engine was on sale in 2018) and a 2012 BMW Série 1 at 0% (its rows were F40
-- engines dated back to 2011-2012). Measured against the Standvirtual snapshot
-- of 2026-11-14 (price_observations), every approved critico and alto entry had
-- the same four kinds of gap:
--
--   A  64 rows had no years. The recommender counts such a row on sale in
--      every year, so a 2019+ BMW 318i counted as an N20 in 2012 and a 2018+
--      Polo DSG as a DQ200 in 2010. Years now come from the snapshot (the
--      variant's listings) or from the generation the row's own verified
--      source page describes. 13 rows stay without years: their source does
--      not say which generation and the snapshot has too few listings.
--   C  48 rows were dated into years in which the market shows the model
--      plenty (20+ listings) and that engine never (same size and power,
--      under any fuel label): those years are cut off. A variant with 10+
--      listings may leave up to 2 stray listings (at most 2%) in the years
--      cut, none next to the years kept. Whole table. The cause was
--      market_aggregator.fill_engine_years, which dated an engine from the
--      listings of its model with the same power whatever their engine size
--      (the 1.5 116d took the years of the 2.0 116d); it now needs the size
--      too. The rows it had dated get their listing count again, by size.
--   B  403 engines for 52 models: the generations in which an approved
--      fault's engine was sold but no catalogue engine was on sale (or none
--      the matcher recognises) get that generation's variants, as v61 and v67
--      did for whole models: listings grouped by fuel, size, power and
--      gearbox; 3+ listings to become a row; years = the listings' years (a
--      lone listing at an edge, cut off by a gap or dwarfed by the next year,
--      dropped when the variant has 10+). All the generation's variants, not
--      only the faulty ones, so the share stays a share.
--        name                    what the listings call the variant
--        reliability_score       the brand's anchor (v68), no editorial deviation
--        maintenance_cost_year   ESTIMATE, v61's rule: brand level + 0.97 EUR/cv
--        consumption, CO2        left empty: the snapshot does not state them
--   D  2 names the matcher could not read: every listing of the Fiat and
--      Abarth 124 Spider 1.4 calls it "MultiAir" (8 and 3 listings).
--
-- Every row records its evidence in `source`. Rows are found by brand, model
-- and code; an update applies only while the row still has the years (or the
-- name) it was planned from, and a row is written only where its years fall
-- in a generation of its model (a database built from the seeds lacks a few
-- older generations production has, e.g. the BMW Série 5 E60). Idempotent.

-- Names carry accents (Série, Citroën, Mégane): read this file as UTF-8 whatever
-- the client's default (psql on Windows otherwise reads it as Windows-1252).
SET client_encoding = 'UTF8';

BEGIN;

-- A: rows without years
UPDATE engines e
SET year_from = v.year_from, year_to = v.year_to,
    source = CASE WHEN length(COALESCE(e.source, '') || '; anos: ' || v.why) <= 200
                  THEN COALESCE(e.source || '; ', '') || 'anos: ' || v.why
                  ELSE left(COALESCE(e.source, ''), 200 - length('; anos: ' || v.why)) || '; anos: ' || v.why END
